body:
The DateCellRenderer applies red text styling to "Yesterday" dates, but the "Overdue Xd" format only starts at 2+ days in the past. This creates an inconsistency where "Yesterday" appears in red but doesn't have an "Overdue" label, while dates 2+ days past show both red styling and "Overdue Xd" label.

Consider updating line 296 to `const isOverdue = isValidDate && diffDays < -1;` to only apply red styling when the date matches the "Overdue Xd" terminology (2-7 days past). You'll need to calculate diffDays in DateCellRenderer similar to formatRelativeDate, or add it as a helper function.
```suggestion
  // Determine if date is overdue (in the past, excluding "Yesterday" to match "Overdue Xd" semantics)
  const date = typeof value === 'string' ? new Date(value) : value;
  const isValidDate = date instanceof Date && !isNaN(date.getTime());
  const startOfToday = new Date();
  startOfToday.setHours(0, 0, 0, 0);
  let diffDays = 0;
  if (isValidDate) {
    // Normalize target date to midnight for day-based comparison
    const dateAtMidnight = new Date(date.getTime());
    dateAtMidnight.setHours(0, 0, 0, 0);
    diffDays = Math.round(
      (dateAtMidnight.getTime() - startOfToday.getTime()) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24),
    );
  }
  const isOverdue = isValidDate && diffDays < -1;
```
